      "text": "To solve the problem, we first need to determine the prime factorization of \\(400\\). We start by dividing \\(400\\) by the smallest prime number, which is \\(2\\):\n\\[\n400 \\div 2 = 200\n\\]\n\\[\n200 \\div 2 = 100\n\\]\n\\[\n100 \\div 2 = 50\n\\]\n\\[\n50 \\div 2 = 25\n\\]\nNext, we divide \\(25\\) by the next smallest prime number, which is \\(5\\):\n\\[\n25 \\div 5 = 5\n\\]\n\\[\n5 \\div 5 = 1\n\\]\nSo, the prime factorization of \\(400\\) is:\n\\[\n400 = 2^4 \\times 5^2\n\\]\nThe sum of the divisors of a number \\(n = p_1^{e_1} \\times p_2^{e_2} \\times \\cdots \\times p_k^{e_k}\\) is given by the formula:\n\\[\n\\sigma(n) = (1 + p_1 + p_1^2 + \\cdots + p_1^{e_1})(1 + p_2 + p_2^2 + \\cdots + p_2^{e_2}) \\cdots (1 + p_k + p_k^2 + \\cdots + p_k^{e_k})\n\\]\nFor \\(400 = 2^4 \\times 5^2\\), the sum of the divisors is:\n\\[\n\\sigma(400) = (1 + 2 + 2^2 + 2^3 + 2^4)(1 + 5 + 5^2)\n\\]\nCalculating each term separately, we get:\n\\[\n1 + 2 + 2^2 + 2^3 + 2^4 = 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16 = 31\n\\]\n\\[\n1 + 5 + 5^2 = 1 + 5 + 25 = 31\n\\]\nTherefore, the sum of the divisors of \\(400\\) is:\n\\[\n\\sigma(400) = 31 \\times 31 = 961\n\\]\nNext, we need to determine the prime factorization of \\(961\\). We start by checking if \\(961\\) is a perfect square:\n\\[\n961 = 31^2\n\\]\nSince \\(961 = 31^2\\), it is a prime number raised to the power of \\(2\\). Therefore, the prime factorization of \\(961\\) is \\(31^2\\), which has only one distinct prime factor, \\(31\\).\n\nThus, the number of distinct prime factors of the sum of the positive divisors of \\(400\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]",
