      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "First, we factorize $400$ into its prime factors:\n\\[400 = 2^4 \\times 5^2.\\]\n\nThe formula for the sum of the divisors of a number $n = p_1^{e_1} \\times p_2^{e_2} \\times \\ldots \\times p_k^{e_k}$ is given by:\n\\[\\sigma(n) = (1 + p_1 + p_1^2 + \\ldots + p_1^{e_1})(1 + p_2 + p_2^2 + \\ldots + p_2^{e_2}) \\ldots (1 + p_k + p_k^2 + \\ldots + p_k^{e_k}).\\]\n\nApplying this formula to $400$, we get:\n\\[\\sigma(400) = (1 + 2 + 2^2 + 2^3 + 2^4)(1 + 5 + 5^2).\\]\n\nNow, we calculate the sum of each geometric series:\n\\begin{align*}\n1 + 2 + 2^2 + 2^3 + 2^4 &= 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16 = 31, \\\\\n1 + 5 + 5^2 &= 1 + 5 + 25 = 31.\n\\end{align*}\n\nThus, the sum of the divisors of $400$ is:\n\\[\\sigma(400) = 31 \\times 31 = 961.\\]\n\nNext, we need to determine the distinct prime factors of $961$:\n\\[961 = 31^2.\\]\n\nThere is only one distinct prime factor in $961$, which is $31$.\n\nTherefore, the number of distinct prime factors of the sum of the divisors of $400$ is $\\boxed{1}$.",
